&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Observations:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RSC Streaming Holds Up Well&lt;/strong&gt;: Even at p99, initial content rendered within 1.4s at steady state. Suspense boundaries prevented waterfall blocking.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Database Connection Pool Becomes Bottleneck&lt;/strong&gt;: At 80 concurrent users, pgBouncer hit 78% saturation. Tasks queued for available connections, spiking p99 to 2.68s.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Memory Growth is Linear&lt;/strong&gt;: paperclipai/paperclip's agent state management scaled predictably—no memory leaks observed over 30-minute sustained load.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;B-Lost Gateway TTFT Consistency&lt;/strong&gt;: When routing LLM requests through B-Lost's relay at &lt;code&gt;https://b-lost.com/v1&lt;/code&gt;, time-to-first-token remained stable (median 380ms) with automatic fallback to backup providers during two brief Claude API timeouts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Production Threshold
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams evaluating paperclipai/paperclip:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Adopt if:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need coordinated multi-agent workflows with &amp;gt;20 concurrent users&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your database can handle connection pooling (recommend 2x expected peak concurrency)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You're already using Next.js App Router and want native RSC streaming&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Avoid if:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your workload is &amp;lt;10 users with simple single-shot LLM calls (overhead isn't justified)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need sub-200ms p99 latency for agent responses (requires distributed task queue like BullMQ)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Configuration Adjustments:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To push beyond 80 concurrent users without latency degradation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Increase pgBouncer pool size&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// config/database.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;poolConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Up from 20&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;idleTimeoutMillis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;connectionTimeoutMillis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Add Redis-backed task queue for async agent jobs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Queue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;bullmq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;agentQueue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Queue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;agent-tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;connection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;host&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;localhost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;port&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6379&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The architecture remains sound—paperclipai/paperclip's design with React Server Components and Server Actions provides clean separation between UI and agent orchestration logic. At our measured throughput of 48 RPS under realistic load, the Next.js runtime handled concurrent agent state without custom locking or race conditions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For latency-sensitive production deployments, pair with a proper task queue and scale database connections proportionally. The framework itself won't be your bottleneck until you're well past 100 concurrent agent workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In my Electron app the main process owns the data. It holds the SQLite database, runs git and talks to the network. The renderer, the React side, never does any of that and holds only a read-only copy of the state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The copy works like this. On connect, the renderer asks for a full snapshot. After that, main sends batches of changes, each with a sequence number, and the renderer applies them in order. Main collects changes for a short moment and sends them as one batch to every window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule I'm glad I made: if a sequence number is skipped, the renderer throws its copy away and takes a fresh snapshot. It never tries to patch the gap. A renderer that is sometimes wrong about the data is worse than one that is briefly empty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost is that there are no optimistic updates. A button sends a named command and waits for the change to come back. Every click has a small delay, so main has to answer fast and batches have to stay small.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I get for it: two windows can't disagree, a command that fails changes nothing on screen, and the renderer has no code that could do something it shouldn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't apply to everything. View state, like which tab is open or a half-typed message, lives in a separate store in the renderer, because nobody else needs to know it and waiting on main for it would be silly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're starting an Electron app, I'd draw the line between "data" and "view" on day one. It's much harder to add later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;⚡ &lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; Test behavior, not implementation, and your suite stops breaking on every refactor. Follow the &lt;strong&gt;testing trophy&lt;/strong&gt; to put effort where it pays, write component tests that survive change, and reserve end-to-end for critical flows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

